<!--
  target_rules.xml -- Wazuh native XML rules for the purple-lab `target` app.

  Hand-written translation of the 4 Sigma rules in docs/sigma-rules/*.yml
  (Task 4). NOT machine-generated: no working `sigma-cli` Wazuh backend
  exists (verified -- see Task 5 report: pysigma-backend-wazuh is not on
  PyPI, is not in `sigma plugin list`'s registry, and is tracked only as
  an open, unimplemented feature request in SigmaHQ/pySigma discussion
  #257 / SigmaHQ/pySigma-plugin-directory discussion #52).

  FIELD-NAME FIDELITY FINDING (bigger than the brief anticipated): the
  brief's flagged risk was that a hypothetical sigma-cli Wazuh backend
  might not preserve the `data.` prefix on field paths. In fact, when
  hand-writing these rules directly, testing via `wazuh-logtest` proved
  the opposite problem: Wazuh's own rule-matching engine addresses
  JSON-decoder dynamic fields WITHOUT the `data.` prefix (e.g. `path`,
  `query_params.q`, `remote_addr`, `form_params.host`) -- that prefix is
  added only by analysisd when *serializing* matched events into
  alerts.json/archives.json output, not in the internal field namespace
  `<field name="...">` matches against. Rules written with `data.`-
  prefixed field names compiled and loaded with no XML errors but
  silently never matched -- confirmed by isolating the base rule (which
  matched fine) from each child field condition (which did not) via
  repeated wazuh-logtest runs. All field references below are therefore
  UNPREFIXED, verified against the real running manager.

  Rule ID plan (local custom range 100000-119999):
    100100  base/parent  - scopes everything below to target's request log
    100101  ALERT        - SQLi on /search               (Sigma 8f6a6b1e-1c3a-4e6a-9b5a-1a2b3c4d5e6f)
    100102  ALERT        - Command injection /admin/diag  (Sigma 7a8b9c0d-2e3f-4a5b-6c7d-8e9f0a1b2c3d)
    100103  base (lvl 0) - single POST /admin/login hit, feeds 100104
    100104  ALERT        - Bruteforce /admin/login         (Sigma 3d2e1f0a-4b5c-6d7e-8f9a-0b1c2d3e4f5a)
    100105  base (lvl 0) - single GET /documents/<id> hit, feeds 100106
    100106  ALERT        - IDOR sequential /documents/<id> (Sigma 5c4d3e2f-1a0b-9c8d-7e6f-5a4b3c2d1e0f)

  Base rules (100100, 100103, 100105) are level="0" by design: level 0
  rules are evaluated and internally tracked for frequency/if_matched_sid
  correlation but are never written to alerts.log/alerts.json themselves.
  This mirrors Wazuh's own shipped ruleset idiom for brute-force detection
  (e.g. 0580-win-security_rules.xml rule 60204, T1110) -- only the
  aggregating rule that crosses the threshold actually alerts.

  Sigma's non-standard `count() by <field> >= N` aggregation syntax used
  in bruteforce-admin-login.yml and idor-documents.yml has no standard
  Sigma grammar and no backend could have converted it correctly. It is
  expressed natively here via Wazuh's own <frequency>/<timeframe>/
  <same_field> rule correlation options (same idiom Wazuh's shipped
  ruleset uses for T1110 brute-force detections, e.g.
  0580-win-security_rules.xml rule 60204/60205, and for arbitrary-field
  grouping, e.g. 0350-amazon_rules.xml rule 80443 `same_field:
  aws.httpRequest.clientIp`). `<same_source_ip/>` was considered but
  rejected: it keys off Wazuh's built-in `srcip` dynamic field, which the
  generic `json` decoder never populates (it has no concept that
  `remote_addr` means source IP) -- confirmed by inspecting
  /var/ossec/ruleset/decoders/0006-json_decoders.xml, which is a bare
  plugin_decoder with no field-role mapping. `<same_field>remote_addr</same_field>`
  is the correct, verified mechanism for this log source.

  ============================================================================
  TASK 8 UPDATE -- native frequency/if_matched_sid correlation never fires
  ============================================================================
  Despite everything above being verified correct (base rules match every
  event, field names are right, same_field is the right primitive), rules
  100104 and 100106 were extensively investigated live (via wazuh-logtest,
  real traffic through the full stack, structural diffing against Wazuh's
  own shipped working example for the exact same idiom --
  0580-win-security_rules.xml rule 60204/60205, which is structurally
  identical to 100103/100104 here -- and a maximally minimal zero-field-
  condition if_matched_sid test rule against our own base rule) and NEVER
  fire. All plausible hypotheses were refuted with concrete evidence. This
  is a real Wazuh 4.9.2 behavior/environment issue, not a rule-authoring
  mistake, and further reverse-engineering of Wazuh's internal correlation
  engine was not a good use of remaining time.

  Decision (explicit direction from the human controller, "the way a real
  SOC/blue team would"): stop relying on Wazuh's native frequency/
  if_matched_sid correlation entirely. The counting now happens in custom
  Active Response scripts (wazuh/active-response/bruteforce-guard.sh,
  wazuh/active-response/idor-guard.sh) that read target's own request log
  directly and do their own threshold/window arithmetic, using the base
  rules below as the trigger.

  This required base rules 100103/100105 to move off level="0": Active
  Response only dispatches off of real alerts, and level-0 rules are
  evaluated for internal correlation bookkeeping only -- they are never
  written to alerts.json and never trigger AR (confirmed against Wazuh 4.9
  docs/behavior before choosing this over any level-0-AR-binding
  alternative, which Wazuh does not support). Both base rules are now
  level="3" -- a real but low-severity alert fires on every single
  attempt, and the custom AR script decides internally whether the actual
  threshold (5 in the original window) has been crossed before doing
  anything for real. See wazuh/config/wazuh_cluster/wazuh_manager.conf's
  Active Response section and the two guard scripts for the rest of this
  design; rules 100104/100106 are left defined below, still expressing the
  original Sigma-rule-derived intent (thresholds, MITRE mapping,
  description) for documentation/traceability, but are no longer bound to
  any Active Response and are not expected to ever appear in alerts.json.
-->
<group name="local,purple_lab_target,">

  <!-- ================= Base: scope to target's web request log ================= -->
  <rule id="100100" level="0">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <location type="pcre2">requests\.jsonl$</location>
    <description>Purple Lab target: web request log event</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================= SQLi on /search (single event) ================= -->
  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="path" type="pcre2">^/search$</field>
    <field name="query_params.q" type="pcre2">(?i)('\s*OR\s*'1'='1|'\s*OR\s*1=1|--|UNION\s+SELECT)</field>
    <description>SQL injection attempt on /search (Sigma: 8f6a6b1e-1c3a-4e6a-9b5a-1a2b3c4d5e6f)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack.initial_access,attack.t1190,sqli,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================= Command injection on /admin/diagnostics (single event) ================= -->
  <rule id="100102" level="15">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="path" type="pcre2">^/admin/diagnostics$</field>
    <field name="method" type="pcre2">^POST$</field>
    <field name="form_params.host" type="pcre2">(;|\||&amp;&amp;|\$\(|`)</field>
    <description>OS command injection attempt on /admin/diagnostics (Sigma: 7a8b9c0d-2e3f-4a5b-6c7d-8e9f0a1b2c3d)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack.execution,attack.t1059,command_injection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================= Bruteforce /admin/login: base + custom AR counting ================= -->
  <!-- Task 8: bumped from level="0" to level="3" (real, low-severity alert
       on EVERY attempt) so Wazuh Active Response has something to bind to.
       bruteforce-guard.sh is bound to this rule (see wazuh_manager.conf)
       and does the real 5-in-120s threshold counting itself, against
       target's own request log, before ever actually locking an account.
       See the file header above for the full why. -->
  <rule id="100103" level="3">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="path" type="pcre2">^/admin/login$</field>
    <field name="method" type="pcre2">^POST$</field>
    <field name="status_code" type="pcre2">^200$</field>
    <field name="form_params.username" type="pcre2">.*</field>
    <description>POST to /admin/login with username parameter present</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>bruteforce_login_base,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Task 8: never fires (see file header) -- native if_matched_sid/
       frequency correlation on Wazuh 4.9.2 does not dispatch this rule
       despite 100103 matching every event. Kept for documentation of the
       original Sigma-derived intent (threshold, window, MITRE mapping)
       only. NOT bound to any Active Response; bruteforce-guard.sh (bound
       to 100103 above) replicates this rule's intent with its own
       counting logic instead. -->
  <rule id="100104" level="10" frequency="5" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>100103</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>remote_addr</same_field>
    <description>Repeated admin login attempts: 5+ POSTs to /admin/login from the same source within 120s -- possible brute force (Sigma: 3d2e1f0a-4b5c-6d7e-8f9a-0b1c2d3e4f5a)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack.credential_access,attack.t1110,bruteforce,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================= IDOR: sequential /documents/<id> probing: base + custom AR counting ================= -->
  <!-- Task 8: bumped from level="0" to level="3" for the same reason as
       100103 above. idor-guard.sh is bound to this rule and does the real
       5-in-60s threshold counting itself before blocking the source IP. -->
  <rule id="100105" level="3">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <field name="path" type="pcre2">^/documents/\d+$</field>
    <field name="method" type="pcre2">^GET$</field>
    <description>GET to /documents/&lt;id&gt;</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <group>idor_probe_base,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Task 8: never fires (see file header) -- same native-correlation
       failure as 100104. Kept for documentation of the original
       Sigma-derived intent only. NOT bound to any Active Response;
       idor-guard.sh (bound to 100105 above) replicates this rule's
       intent with its own counting logic instead. -->
  <rule id="100106" level="7" frequency="5" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>100105</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>remote_addr</same_field>
    <description>Sequential document ID probing (IDOR): 5+ requests to /documents/&lt;id&gt; from the same source within 60s (Sigma: 5c4d3e2f-1a0b-9c8d-7e6f-5a4b3c2d1e0f)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1213</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>attack.discovery,attack.t1213,idor,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
